{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And skins of tachashim.</b> A species of animal. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in the chapter 'With what may one light' (Shabbat 28a): the tachash that existed in the days of Moses was a creature unto itself; it had one horn on its forehead, and for that hour it was provided to Moses, and he made from it the Mishkan, and it was hidden away. That is to say, those tachashim that were available in the desert were available only for the need of the Mishkan alone, and for the honor of the Holy One, blessed be He, their skins were taken, which were painted with wondrous and superior coloring, and afterward they disappeared. And our Rabbis of blessed memory made a precise inference from the fact that they said 'it had one horn on its forehead' — conclude from this that it was pure, for we say: the ox that the first man sacrificed had one horn on its forehead, as is said (Psalms 69:32) ״ותיטב לה' משור פר מקרן מפריס״ — מקרן מפריס, two horns, implies מקרן, one horn, is written. And furthermore, that nothing was fit for heavenly work except a pure animal alone, as is said (Exodus 13:9): ״למען תהיה תורת ה' בפיך״ — from what is permitted in your mouth. And it had many colors, and therefore Onkelos translated ססגונא — proud in its colors." "<b>And acacia wood.</b> From where did they have them in the desert? Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: Jacob our father foresaw by the holy spirit that Israel were destined to build a Mishkan in the desert, and he brought cedars to Egypt and planted them there, and commanded his sons to take them along when they went out of Egypt."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:57.364577402Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}